<content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Use the patterns you notice to complete the tables. Show your reasoning. Complete your work on paper. Then select the
  <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Table A</para>
    <table>
      <tgroup cols="7"><thead/>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>\(x\)</entry>
          <entry>
          0
          </entry>
          <entry>
           1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
           25
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>\(y\)</entry>
          <entry>
            2.5
          </entry>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry>
            17.5
          </entry>
          <entry>
            25
          </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </tgroup></table>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <para>Table A: 32.5 when \(x\) is 4, and 190 (or \(2.5+25 \cdot 7.5\)) when \(x\) is 25.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Table B</para>
    <table>
      <tgroup cols="7"><thead/>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>\(x\)</entry>
          <entry>
          0
          </entry>
          <entry>
           1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
           25
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>\(y\)</entry>
          <entry>	
            2.5
          </entry>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry>
            40
          </entry>
          <entry>
            160
          </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </tgroup></table>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <para>Table B: 640 when \(x\) is 4, and \(2.5 \cdot 4^{25}\) when \(x\) is 25.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
